 picture of  who the winner was  however it could take weeks to form a  government  let's take a look at what this is likely  to mean with marcus carlson who's a  presenter and reporter for tv4 in  stockholm welcome to dw  does a right wheat  right leaning government that includes a  far right to party mean that sweden will  necessarily lurch to the right  i mean it's clear that the sweden  democrats will have more influence than  they ever had before with the more than  20 percent  of the vote exactly what their influence  will be well the jury's still out on  that because now uh these parties of the  right that have won the election they  have to uh strike a coalition agreement  just like in germany and many other  countries how long that will take is  unclear at the moment and how difficult  and arduous that process is also unclear  at the moment what is clear or what  appears fairly clear is that the sweden  democrats actually won't be part of the  government why is that well because the  liberal party also part of this winning  coalition has said that it won't accept  the sweden democrats within an actual  government so what it is likely is some  sort of uh supply and confidence  agreement between the sweden democrats  and the other parties of the right-wing  coalition but as i say it's  difficult to say exactly what kind of  influence and what kind of relationship  these parties will have going forward  okay so no sweden democrats  in government  but the uh prime minister of the third  largest party in parliament is going to  have to rely  on votes  from the sweden democrats in order to  push his agenda through so how does he  do that how does he convince a larger  party to go his way  well  there are certain points  over which these parties agree that  includes energy these parties all want  to build more nuclear power for instance  it includes defense issues it includes  certain migration issues um so there are  um  some  common points here that these partie